  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Moderate tidal flooding persists, with accumulating \nmountain snow this afternoon into tonight.\n\nA potent and large area of low pressure over the central \nAppalachians this morning is going to steadily track north/east \ntoday, eventually pushing away from the area tonight into the \nweekend. The tight surface pressure gradient still in place and \noverall strength of the low will result in gusty conditions this \nmorning and afternoon. This time winds are going to be out of the \nsouthwest, but certainly not as strong as yesterday. Peak gusts this \nafternoon are going to be around 25-35 mph, and up to 40-50 mph in \nthe highest ridges of the Alleghenies. Seasonal temperatures in the \n40s to mid 50s as abundant cloud cover eventually starts to clear \nlate this afternoon to evening. \n\nA Winter Weather Advisory has been issued for western Grant and \nwestern Pendleton Counties from this afternoon through Saturday \nmorning. In most cases where southwesterly flow prevails the snow \ntotals usually underperform in the mountains since there isn't \nenough moisture. However, the area of low pressure over the OH \nValley remains potent and has ample low-level moisture feeding \norographically forced showers in the mountains. Marginal surface \ntemperatures through this afternoon result in a rain/snow mix that \neventually transitions to all snow late afternoon into tonight. \nAround 3 inches of snow is likely at elevations above 3000 feet, and \nsome locally higher amounts up to 4 inches are possible. At lower \nelevations down to 1000 feet a coasting to an inch or two is \npossible. As the area of low pressure departs tonight and warmer air \nmoves in aloft, a light freezing drizzle develops Saturday morning \nin the Advisory area before precip comes to an end. \n\nElsewhere, dry conditions are expected Saturday as ample sunshine \nreturns. Slightly warmer temperatures in the 50s to low 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another potent area of low pressure brings \nprecipitation Sunday night into Monday. \n\nHigh pressure will keep the region dry Sunday. Temperatures will be \nnear seasonable with highs middle 50s to lower 60s.\n\nAs the high pushes further to the southeast Sunday night, a low \npressure system will move from the middle Mississippi Valley on \nSunday toward the eastern Great Lakes Sunday night. A trailing cold \nfront will push across the region Sunday night into Monday morning.\nShowers will likely accompany the front. We can't rule out a clap of \nthunder or two either given that there will be plenty of wind shear, \ndespite instability being minimal to nil and the time of night for \nfrontal passage. Highs cooler Sunday with highs mainly in lower to \nupper 50s.\n\nOnce the front moves to the east and southeast Monday afternoon, \nhigh pressure will become reestablished and bring dry conditions \nand seasonable temperatures to the region Monday through \nWednesday.",
